🎯 Core Concept: Reverse Proxy Architecture (Nginx ↔ Unix Socket ↔ Gunicorn ↔ systemd)
Internet (port 443, HTTPS)
      │
      ▼
  Nginx  ──── public-facing, terminates SSL
      │  (proxy_pass unix:/opt/myapp/myapp.sock)
      ▼
 Gunicorn ──── actual WSGI server, runs the app
      │  (managed by)
      ▼
 systemd ──── keeps the Gunicorn process alive

Why a Unix socket instead of a TCP port? The socket file (myapp.sock) is only accessible inside the server — no extra network port is opened, which reduces the attack surface and is marginally faster (bypasses the network stack).

Why is systemd necessary? If Gunicorn crashes or the server reboots, Restart=always automatically brings the process back up — without it, you'd have to SSH in and restart it manually every time.

  Other Concepts — Their Specific Role in This Project
Concept	Where it's used in this project	Exam-important point
Users / Permissions	The myapp system user (non-root) runs the app; socket file permissions control whether Nginx (usually the www-data group) can read that socket	chmod 660, chown myapp:www-data — running the app as root is a security risk
systemctl / apt	daemon-reload (load a new unit file), enable --now (auto-start on boot + start immediately), apt install nginx certbot	Difference between enable and start — enable = will run on boot, start = runs now; they are independent
Ports / ufw	Only ports 22 (SSH), 80, and 443 are open — everything else is closed	sudo ufw allow 'Nginx Full' opens both 80 and 443 in one command
DNS A-record	Maps the domain → server IP; certbot won't work without this	Certbot's HTTP-01 challenge requires the domain to actually resolve to the server, otherwise the certificate request fails
SSH key-based login	Used for server access; disabling password login is a best practice	PasswordAuthentication no in /etc/ssh/sshd_config — protects against brute-force attacks
Python venv	Each project gets its own isolated dependency set (/opt/myapp/venv)	Doesn't touch the system Python — avoids version conflicts with other projects on the same server
certbot / TLS	certbot --nginx edits the Nginx config automatically and sets up a systemd timer for auto-renewal (certs expire every 90 days)	certbot renew --dry-run — testing renewal without actually renewing is a common exam question
Bash set -euo pipefail	At the top of both deploy.sh and backup.sh	-e = exit on any error, -u = fail on use of an undefined variable, -o pipefail = if any command in a pipe fails, the whole pipeline fails
cron	Used to run backup.sh automatically every day	0 2 * * * = every day at 2 AM — cron syntax is minute hour day month weekday
git clone vs pull	clone = download the full repo for the first time; pull = update an existing repo (fetch + merge)	deploy.sh only uses git pull — clone is only needed once, during initial setup
